---
import PublicContentLayout from '../layouts/PublicContentLayout.astro';
import { product } from '../data/product';
import { releaseAnchor, releaseHistory } from '../data/releases';

export const prerender = true;

const title = 'Reinstate Changelog and Release Notes';
const description =
  `Read Reinstate release notes, including ${product.currentRelease} compatibility, path mapping, restore verification, security, and installer changes.`;
const currentReleaseDate = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en', {
  year: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  day: 'numeric',
  timeZone: 'UTC',
}).format(new Date(`${product.currentReleaseDate}T00:00:00Z`));
const answer =
  `The latest published Reinstate tag is ${product.currentRelease}, dated ${currentReleaseDate}. ` +
  `${product.currentRelease} is a release candidate that adds Phase 4 structured cross-agent handoff on top of stable ${product.stableRelease}; its dual-platform tagged-artifact acceptance is pending, and stable support remains ${product.stableRelease}.`;
---

<PublicContentLayout
  {title}
  {description}
  path="/changelog"
  eyebrow="Changelog"
  {answer}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Changelog', path: '/changelog' },
  ]}
  tags={['release notes', product.currentRelease, 'changelog', 'Reinstate releases']}
>
  <h2 id="unreleased">Unreleased</h2>
  <p>
    Current unreleased website work adds the SEO, answer-engine, AI-search, and social-preview
    foundation and introduces signed <code>website-vYYYY.MM.DD.N</code> deployment tags. A website
    tag can publish reviewed site changes from current <code>main</code> while the public CLI and
    installer routes remain pinned to the separately signed <code>{product.currentRelease}</code>
    release. It is not a new Reinstate version or evidence for any platform outside the documented
    release boundary.
  </p>
  <p>
    The repository's
    <a href="https://github.com/HarjjotSinghh/reinstate/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md"
      >canonical CHANGELOG.md</a
    > is authoritative if this summary and the current branch differ.
  </p>

  <h2 id={releaseAnchor(product.currentRelease)}>
    {product.currentRelease} · {currentReleaseDate}
  </h2>
  <h3>Structured cross-agent handoff</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>rein handoff</code> and <code>rein resume --with</code> start a <em>new</em> Claude Code
      or Codex session that continues the same task. This is not a resume of the source session.
    </li>
    <li>
      Claude Code, Codex CLI, Gemini CLI, OpenCode, and Grok Build can be handoff sources. Gemini
      CLI, OpenCode, and Grok Build are source-only; Claude Code and Codex are the only
      destinations.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each capsule component is labeled <code>exact</code>, <code>normalized</code>,
      <code>summarized</code>, <code>referenced</code>, or <code>omitted</code>, so lossy transfer
      stays visible rather than implied to be complete.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Fixes carried since v0.4.0-rc.2</h3>
  <p>
    <code>v0.4.0-rc.2</code> was published for testing and <strong>failed</strong> its physical
    dual-platform acceptance. This candidate carries the product fixes for what that run found.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A structured handoff from a different Git repository than the source session is refused
      (C4). Same-repo subdirectories remain allowed.
    </li>
    <li>
      Destination launch without a real TTY fails closed with <code>ErrNonInteractiveLaunch</code>
      / exit <code>7</code> before LookPath or child spawn (F8).
    </li>
    <li>
      Grok, Gemini, and OpenCode are valid source agents for the busy check, so a Grok-sourced
      dry-run no longer exits <code>1</code> with <code>unsupported agent "grok"</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A timed-out version probe classifies as <code>UNTESTED</code> / Compatibility (exit
      <code>5</code>), never Runtime or absent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>--no-redact</code> is a known flag for Codex and Claude sources; Grok still refuses
      it with exit <code>2</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      With vendor isolation variables set, <code>rein list</code> does not show operator-home
      sessions.
    </li>
    <li>
      Checkpoint policy sidecar/events contain references only, not verbatim conversation
      bodies, and output includes <code>projection_events</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Removing a destination MCP or skill the source still has produces Missing +
      <code>handoff.capability.*</code> warning IDs; Execute without acknowledging those IDs
      does not launch.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Fixes carried since v0.4.0-rc.1</h3>
  <p>
    <code>v0.4.0-rc.1</code> was published for testing and <strong>failed</strong> its physical
    acceptance. This candidate carries the fixes for what that run found.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A Claude-sourced handoff no longer requires a version file that real installations never
      create. Source version now resolves through the same probe <code>rein inspect</code> uses, so
      Claude-sourced handoffs stop exiting <code>5</code> on every real install.
    </li>
    <li>
      Transcript readers tokenize the paths they emit, so a session that had read a file no longer
      fails capsule validation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>changed_files</code> is populated from the live working tree. Every earlier handoff told
      the destination <code>Changed files: (none)</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A version probe that times out is classified as a failed measurement rather than an absent
      agent, so a determinable out-of-range agent is refused instead of silently accepted.
    </li>
    <li>
      Capsule validation checks path-typed fields instead of every string, so a message beginning
      with a slash command no longer aborts the handoff.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Continuity and privacy boundary</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Native execution remains same-vendor; Reinstate does not translate transcripts across agents.
    </li>
    <li>
      Handoff parses the source locally with no source model call, and never writes vendor-internal
      session files.
    </li>
    <li>
      Capsules stay owner-only under <code>$REINSTATE_HOME/handoffs/</code> and are hard-excluded
      from sync.
    </li>
    <li>
      Stable <code>{product.stableRelease}</code> verified resume, local continuity, and encrypted
      sync remain available.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Acceptance boundary</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Dual-platform tagged-artifact acceptance on Apple Silicon macOS and native Windows x64 is
      pending for this candidate. <code>v0.4.0-rc.1</code> and <code>v0.4.0-rc.2</code> were
      published and failed that acceptance.
    </li>
    <li>
      Intel macOS and Linux/WSL2 remain optional, unsupported/unverified evidence and do not block
      this candidate.
    </li>
    <li>
      Passing this candidate does not authorize stable <code>v0.4.0</code>; that requires a
      separate reviewed promotion and fresh validation.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href={product.currentReleaseUrl}>View the {product.currentRelease} tag on GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="release-history">Release-candidate history</h2>
  <div class="pc-table-wrap">
    <table class="pc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Release</th>
          <th scope="col">Date</th>
          <th scope="col">Primary focus</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          releaseHistory.map((release) => {
            const anchor = releaseAnchor(release.version);
            return (
              <tr id={release.version === product.currentRelease ? undefined : anchor}>
                <td><a href={`#${anchor}`}>{release.version}</a></td>
                <td><time datetime={release.date}>{release.date}</time></td>
                <td>{release.summary}</td>
              </tr>
            );
          })
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="rc1-foundation">What the first release candidate established</h2>
  <p>
    RC1 introduced the Cobra CLI, versioned local state, device detection, diagnostics, verified
    installers, S3-compatible storage, age encryption, path mapping, manifests, push and pull,
    conflicts, Claude Code and Codex adapters, native keyring credentials, restore backups, and the
    synthetic compatibility-test foundation. It also established <code>rein</code> as the short
    alias for the <code>reinstate</code> binary.
  </p>

  <h2 id="versioning">How to read pre-1.0 release notes</h2>
  <p>
    Release candidates were published for testing before stable v0.1 certification, which v0.1.0 completed. Read every
    intermediate release note before replacing a binary because compatibility behavior, session
    artifact formats, setup validation, and release gates may still change.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Pin an explicit release in automated setup.</li>
    <li>Verify checksums before installing.</li>
    <li>Run <code>rein setup check</code> after agent or Reinstate upgrades.</li>
    <li>Use pull dry-runs and maintain independent backups of important work.</li>
  </ul>

  <section class="pc-related" aria-labelledby="related-heading">
    <h2 id="related-heading">Release resources</h2>
    <nav aria-label="Related pages">
      <a href={product.tagsUrl}>All source tags</a>
      <a href="/compatibility">Compatibility matrix</a>
      <a href="/docs/getting-started">Install Reinstate</a>
      <a href="/open-source">Source and contributions</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
</PublicContentLayout>
